**user** (2023-05-22 4:32 AM): I need two input fields one is called MID and the other is called Descriptor. They are side by side in HTML.

**assistant** (2023-05-22 4:32 AM): You can create two input fields side by side in HTML using a combination of HTML and CSS. Here is an example of how to do it:
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
	<title>Two Input Fields Side by Side</title>
	<style>
		form {
			display: flex;
			flex-direction: row;
			align-items: center;
		}

		label {
			margin-right: 10px;
		}
	</style>
</head>
<body>
	<form>
		<label for="MID">MID:</label>
		<input type="text" id="MID" name="MID">
		<label for="Descriptor">Descriptor:</label>
		<input type="text" id="Descriptor" name="Descriptor">
	</form>
</body>
</html>
```
In this example, the form is styled using CSS `flexbox` to display the input fields side by side. The `label` element is used to provide a text label for each input field. The `for` attribute of the `label` element is used to associate the label with the corresponding input field using the `id` attribute.

You can modify the styling to suit your specific requirements.

**user** (2023-05-22 4:32 AM): Now I'd like to be able to press a button to clone those two input fields if the user needs to add more rows

**assistant** (2023-05-22 4:32 AM): You can use JavaScript to add a button that allows the user to clone the input fields if they need to add more rows. Here is an example of how to do it:
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
	<title>Clone Input Fields</title>
	<style>
		form {
			display: flex;
			flex-direction: row;
			align-items: center;
		}

		label {
			margin-right: 10px;
		}

		#button-container {
			margin-top: 10px;
		}

		#add-row-button {
			margin-right: 10px;
		}
	</style>
</head>
<body>
	<form>
		<div class="input-container">
			<label for="MID">MID:</label>
			<input type="text" id="MID" name="MID">
			<label for="Descriptor">Descriptor:</label>
			<input type="text" id="Descriptor" name="Descriptor">
		</div>
		<div id="button-container">
			<button id="add-row-button" type="button">Add Row</button>
		</div>
	</form>

	<script>
		// Select the button and the input container
		const addRowButton = document.getElementById("add-row-button");
		const inputContainer = document.querySelector(".input-container");

		// Add event listener to the button
		addRowButton.addEventListener("click", function() {
			// Clone the input container
			const clonedInputContainer = inputContainer.cloneNode(true);

			// Clear the values of the cloned input fields
			const clonedInputs = clonedInputContainer.querySelectorAll("input");
			clonedInputs.forEach(function(input) {
				input.value = "";
			});

			// Insert the cloned input container after the original input container
			inputContainer.parentNode.insertBefore(clonedInputContainer, inputContainer.nextSibling);
		});
	</script>
</body>
</html>
```
In this example, a button with the id `add-row-button` is added below the input fields. When the button is clicked, JavaScript code is executed that clones the `div` element with the class `input-container`, which contains the two input fields. The cloned `div` element is then inserted after the original `div` element.
